"An RDF graph depicts statements as subject-predicate-object triples with resources and literal values distinguished.\n\nUse when: Explain linked data assertions and shared resource identity. Review a small RDF dataset or query pattern.\n\nAvoid when: The objective is a property-graph schema or ontology restriction model.\n\nVisual grammar: Resource nodes identify IRIs or explicitly scoped blank nodes. Directed predicate edges connect subjects to objects. Literal nodes include relevant datatype or language tags.\n\nBuild: Select a bounded set of asserted or inferred triples. Declare prefixes and expand ambiguous identifiers in a key. Reuse one node for each resource identity. Place literals near their subject and route shared-resource edges around labels.\n\nCheck: Literals never appear as subjects in an ordinary RDF graph. Predicate direction preserves each triple’s meaning. Asserted facts and inferred statements are clearly distinguished. IRI labels, datatype annotations, and connectors do not overlap.\n\nPitfalls: Treating identical display names as identical resource identifiers. Omitting datatype or language information when it changes meaning.\n\nExample: book:42 has creator person:7 and a language-tagged title; person:7 has an explicit type assertion.\n\nAlternatives: Ontology diagram, Property graph schema diagram."
